    [
        {
            "type": "doc_title",
            "bbox": [
                0.043,
                0.174,
                0.701,
                0.252
            ],
            "angle": 0,
            "content": "Four Priorities for Defense Ministries"
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "bbox": [
                0.042,
                0.281,
                0.914,
                0.302
            ],
            "angle": 0,
            "content": "European defense ministries can likewise take specific steps to increase defense production.",
            "merge_prev": false
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "bbox": [
                0.041,
                0.316,
                0.935,
                0.415
            ],
            "angle": 0,
            "content": "Collaborate to fast-track designs and pool industrial resources. Governments must determine which capabilities to develop for their country's own use and which to develop through alliances with other EU nations. Collective land-based air defense, for example, will get more assets into the field faster and avoid the interoperability issues that occur when countries buy highly customized solutions.",
            "merge_prev": false
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "bbox": [
                0.041,
                0.43,
                0.95,
                0.549
            ],
            "angle": 0,
            "content": "Don't chase perfection. All countries want the best defense systems they can get. But in some cases, extra features and capabilities may not be worth the time it takes to add them. Instead, given the urgent need for speed and volume, ministries can identify those platforms for which “good enough” solutions are acceptable. That may mean accepting components that are made to stock or scaling back some functionality, provided these tradeoffs don’t sacrifice operational capabilities or readiness.",
            "merge_prev": false
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "bbox": [
                0.041,
                0.563,
                0.946,
                0.662
            ],
            "angle": 0,
            "content": "Similarly, ministries should minimize design changes once production starts. That does not mean locking in designs permanently. In some cases, a design change may be operationally necessary, and governments may be willing to wait for it to be implemented. But contractors can only benefit from the experience curve when designs remain stable for reasonably long production runs.",
            "merge_prev": false
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "bbox": [
                0.041,
                0.676,
                0.947,
                0.797
            ],
            "angle": 0,
            "content": "Provide predictable demand signals to industry. Contractors and suppliers will only invest in new capacity if they have a clear view to future demand. Defense ministries can shift to multiyear contracts, purchasing agreements with a minimum guaranteed volume, and rolling, five- to ten-year commitments. In addition, they can create more demand transparency by developing and communicating explicit, up-to-date demand pipelines. These measures give contractors and suppliers the confidence to devote funds to capex, hiring, upskilling, and other investments.",
            "merge_prev": false
        },
        {
            "type": "text",
            "bbox": [
                0.041,
                0.809,
                0.953,
                0.89
            ],
            "angle": 0,
            "content": "Streamline regulations. Governments can remove some of the regulatory burden that slows down manufacturing. For example, defense ministries can make contracting terms less complex, reducing the burden on small players, accelerating the tender process, and potentially creating a larger pool of suppliers.",
            "merge_prev": false
        },
        {
